    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/chess.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">chess</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/cooking.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">cooking</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/gardening.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">gardening</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/fishing.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">fishing</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/yoga.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">yoga</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/tennis.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">tennis</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/table-tennis.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">table tennis</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/volleyball.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">volleyball</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/golf.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">golf</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/skiing.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">skiing</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/dancer.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">dancing</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/airplane.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">traveling</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/board-games.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">board games</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A fictional community-center profile</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">“Rina plays chess on Thursdays and goes hiking once a month. Sam does yoga on Saturday mornings. Lee reads books in the quiet room. Rina enjoys playing chess, and she’d like to join the photography walk next week.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which activities use <span class="ex">play</span>, <span class="ex">go</span>, and <span class="ex">do</span>? Which activity uses its own ordinary verb?</li>
      <li>What form follows <span class="ex">enjoys</span>? What two words follow the contraction <span class="ex">she’d like</span>?</li>
      <li>Which sentences describe a routine? Which one describes present interest in a future activity?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected fixed activity chunks and forms from routine, experience, and present-interest meanings.">
    <h3>Discovery check: complete the profile</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Choose the whole chunk from the stated activity and meaning.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Activity profile choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="plays">plays</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="goes">goes</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="does">does</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reads">reads</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="playing">playing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to join">to join</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Rina’s Thursday routine uses the game chunk: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="plays" data-hint="Chess uses the fixed chunk play chess; she adds s." data-fix="Rina plays chess on Thursdays." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> chess on Thursdays.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Her monthly outdoor outing uses a go plus -ing chunk: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="goes" data-hint="Hiking commonly forms the outing chunk go hiking; she adds es to go." data-fix="She goes hiking once a month." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> hiking once a month.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Sam’s Saturday practice uses the fixed yoga chunk: Sam <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="does" data-hint="Yoga uses the fixed chunk do yoga; Sam takes does." data-fix="Sam does yoga on Saturday mornings." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> yoga on Saturday mornings.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Lee’s quiet activity uses its own ordinary verb: Lee <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reads" data-hint="Reading uses the ordinary verb read, not play, go, or do." data-fix="Lee reads books in the quiet room." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> books in the quiet room.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Rina experiences chess positively: She enjoys <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="playing" data-hint="Enjoy requires the -ing form of the following activity verb." data-fix="Rina enjoys playing chess." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> chess.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>She expresses present interest in a future photography walk: She’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to join" data-hint="Would like takes to plus the base verb for a present wish or polite interest." data-fix="She’d like to join the photography walk next week." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the photography walk next week.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: learn the whole activity chunk</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Play</span>, <span class="ex">go</span>, and <span class="ex">do</span> are useful patterns, but they are not a machine that creates every activity phrase. Learn the activity with its normal verb.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>chunk family</th><th>useful tendency</th><th>natural examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>play + game, sport, or instrument</b></td><td>games with rules, many sports, and musical instruments</td><td><span class="ex">play chess · play soccer · play tennis · play the guitar</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>go + -ing activity</b></td><td>some established outings and movement activities</td><td><span class="ex">go hiking · go swimming · go running · go fishing</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>do + activity</b></td><td>some practices, training, and fixed activity names</td><td><span class="ex">do yoga · do martial arts · do a workout</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>another ordinary verb</b></td><td>many activities have their own main verb</td><td><span class="ex">read books · watch movies · cook · garden · paint</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Go plus -ing is not a general rule for every -ing word.</strong> Say <span class="ex">go hiking</span>, but <span class="ex">read books</span>, <span class="ex">watch movies</span>, <span class="ex">listen to music</span>, <span class="ex">take photos</span>, and <span class="ex">garden</span>. Do not create <span class="ex">go reading</span>, <span class="ex">play photography</span>, or <span class="ex">do movies</span>.</p>

  <h3>Useful U.S. chunks and small-word boundaries</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>meaning</th><th>natural U.S. chunk</th><th>boundary</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>sports and games</b></td><td><span class="ex">play soccer · play tennis · play chess · play video games</span></td><td>no article before these activity names</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>instruments</b></td><td><span class="ex">play the guitar · play the piano</span></td><td><span class="ex">the</span> is the reliable A1 instrument pattern</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>screen activities</b></td><td><span class="ex">watch a movie · watch movies · watch TV</span></td><td>one movie needs <span class="ex">a</span>; general <span class="ex">TV</span> has no article</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>audio activities</b></td><td><span class="ex">listen to music · listen to a podcast</span></td><td><span class="ex">listen</span> needs <span class="ex">to</span> before the audio</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>photos</b></td><td><span class="ex">take photos · take pictures · do photography</span></td><td><span class="ex">take</span> names the individual action</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>bicycles</b></td><td><span class="ex">ride a bike · go biking · bike</span></td><td>three natural viewpoints for the activity</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>physical practice</b></td><td><span class="ex">exercise · work out · do a workout · do some exercises</span></td><td>general U.S. English normally avoids bare <span class="ex">do exercise</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>In U.S. English, <span class="ex">movie</span> is a common everyday word. <span class="ex">Go to the movies</span> means go to a movie theater. For time, use <span class="ex">on weekends</span> for a repeated pattern, <span class="ex">on the weekend</span> for a particular weekend in many U.S. contexts, and no preposition before <span class="ex">this weekend</span>.</p>

  <h3>Build the -ing spelling accurately</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>verb shape</th><th>change</th><th>examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>most verbs</b></td><td>add <b>-ing</b></td><td><span class="ex">cook → cooking · read → reading · play → playing</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>final silent e</b></td><td>drop <b>e</b>, then add <b>-ing</b></td><td><span class="ex">dance → dancing · ride → riding</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>short vowel + final consonant</b></td><td>double the consonant in these short forms</td><td><span class="ex">run → running · swim → swimming</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">This is retrieval from the <a href="/lessons/a1/present-continuous/">present continuous spelling system</a>. Here the same <span class="ex">-ing</span> form can name an activity after <span class="ex">enjoy</span> or <span class="ex">like</span>.</p>

  <h2>One activity, five different messages</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The verb form changes because the speaker’s message changes. Compare the same activity, <span class="ex">play tennis</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>message</th><th>form</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>routine or fact</b></td><td><b>present simple</b></td><td><span class="ex">I play tennis on Saturdays.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>preference or enjoyment</b></td><td><b>like/love + -ing</b></td><td><span class="ex">I like playing tennis.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>experienced enjoyment</b></td><td><b>enjoy + -ing</b></td><td><span class="ex">I enjoy playing tennis.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>ability</b></td><td><b>can + base verb</b></td><td><span class="ex">I can play tennis.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>present wish or polite interest</b></td><td><b>would like + to + base verb</b></td><td><span class="ex">I’d like to play tennis this Saturday.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Like</span> and <span class="ex">love</span> can also take <span class="ex">to + verb</span>: <span class="ex">I like to swim</span> and <span class="ex">I love to cook</span> are natural. At this level, <span class="ex">like swimming</span> presents the activity as an experience, while <span class="ex">like to swim</span> can present a preferred activity or habit. The meanings often overlap. <strong>Enjoy is different:</strong> use <span class="ex">enjoy swimming</span>, not <span class="ex">enjoy to swim</span>.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Would like</span> is not the same as ordinary <span class="ex">like</span>. <span class="ex">I like playing chess</span> states a preference. <span class="ex">I’d like to play chess today</span> expresses a present wish or polite interest in a possible action.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected forms for experience, preference, ability, present interest, and third-person routine.">
    <h3>Drill 1: choose the form from the message</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Activity form choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="playing">playing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="play">play</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to play">to play</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reading">reading</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reads">reads</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to join">to join</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The profile describes chess as an enjoyed experience: Rina enjoys <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="playing" data-hint="Enjoy requires the -ing form of the following activity verb." data-fix="Rina enjoys playing chess." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> chess.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The coach asks about ability, not preference: Can Rina <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="play" data-hint="After can, use the base verb without to or -ing." data-fix="Can Rina play chess?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> chess?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Rina expresses polite interest in today’s game: She’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to play" data-hint="Would like takes to plus the base verb for a present wish." data-fix="She’d like to play chess today." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> chess today.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The profile uses the activity-as-experience pattern after ordinary like: Lee likes <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reading" data-hint="The selected pattern is like plus -ing, so use reading." data-fix="Lee likes reading short stories." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> short stories.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The schedule describes Lee’s repeated Tuesday routine: Lee <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reads" data-hint="A present-simple routine with Lee takes reads." data-fix="Lee reads short stories on Tuesdays." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> short stories on Tuesdays.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>A visitor expresses present interest in a future group: I’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to join" data-hint="Would like takes to plus the base verb join." data-fix="I’d like to join the book club." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the book club.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Ask the question that matches your purpose</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>natural question</th><th>possible answer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>discover activities</b></td><td><span class="ex">What do you do in your free time?</span></td><td><span class="ex">I read and play chess.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>discover preferences</b></td><td><span class="ex">What do you like doing in your free time?</span></td><td><span class="ex">I like reading.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>check one preference</b></td><td><span class="ex">Do you like playing chess?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Yes, I do. / No, I don’t.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>ask about frequency</b></td><td><span class="ex">How often do you play?</span></td><td><span class="ex">About once a week.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>ask about ability</b></td><td><span class="ex">Can you play?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Yes, I can. / No, I can’t.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>check availability</b></td><td><span class="ex">Are you free this Saturday?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Yes, I am. / Sorry, I’m not.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Free time</span> names time available for chosen activities. <span class="ex">Are you free?</span> asks whether someone is available at a particular time. It does not ask which hobbies the person has.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched each question to activities, preferences, frequency, or availability.">
    <h3>Drill 2: identify the question’s purpose</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Question purpose choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="activities">activities</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="preferences">preferences</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="frequency">frequency</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="availability">availability</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span><span class="ex">What do you do in your free time?</span> asks about <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="activities" data-hint="What do you do asks which actions form the person’s free-time routine." data-fix="What do you do in your free time? asks about activities." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">What do you like doing?</span> asks about <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="preferences" data-hint="Like asks which activities the person enjoys." data-fix="What do you like doing? asks about preferences." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">How often do you play chess?</span> asks about <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="frequency" data-hint="How often asks how frequently an activity happens." data-fix="How often do you play chess? asks about frequency." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">Are you free this Saturday?</span> asks about <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="availability" data-hint="Be free at a stated time means be available then." data-fix="Are you free this Saturday? asks about availability." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: connected questions and clear activity words</h3>
    <p>Keep the grammar opening light and stress the activity: <span class="ex">Do you like <strong>PLAY</strong>ing chess?</span> In relaxed U.S. speech, <span class="ex">do you</span> links closely and may sound like <span class="ex">d’you</span> or <span class="ex">juh</span>. <span class="ex">What do you</span> may sound approximately like <span class="ex">whaddaya</span>. These are listening guides, not spellings to write.</p>
    <p>Keep the final nasal sound in <span class="ex">-ing</span> without adding a separate hard <span class="ex">g</span>: <span class="ex"><strong>PLAY</strong>ing · <strong>READ</strong>ing · <strong>SWIM</strong>ming</span>. Link activity chunks: <span class="ex">go-<strong>HI</strong>king · listen-to-<strong>MU</strong>sic · play-the gui-<strong>TAR</strong></span>.</p>
    <p>Common stress patterns include <span class="ex"><strong>HOB</strong>by · <strong>VID</strong>eo games · pho-<strong>TOG</strong>-ra-phy · gui-<strong>TAR</strong></span>. Keep third-person endings audible: <span class="ex">likes</span> ends /s/, <span class="ex">plays</span> ends /z/, and <span class="ex">watches</span> adds a final syllable /ɪz/.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read routine, preference, ability, present-interest, question-purpose, and availability listening check">
