  <h2>Use it safely and flexibly</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real interests, abilities, body, health, schedule, work, family, friends, location, finances, religion, devices, travel, or weekend plans. Use the fictional profiles and activity guide, invent details, or share personal information only when you freely choose.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Build the listings:</b> Use the fictional guide to make complete chunks with <span class="ex">play</span>, <span class="ex">go</span>, <span class="ex">do</span>, <span class="ex">read</span>, <span class="ex">listen to</span>, and <span class="ex">take</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Separate the messages:</b> Use one activity to state a routine, a preference with <span class="ex">enjoy</span>, an ability with <span class="ex">can</span>, and present interest with <span class="ex">would like to</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Interview a fictional visitor:</b> Ask about activities, preferences, frequency, ability, and Saturday availability. The teacher answers from a supplied fictional profile.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Offer real choice:</b> Compare an indoor, outdoor, online, quiet, social, low-equipment, or flexible-movement option without assuming what the visitor can or should do.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Final production: recommend two fictional community-center activities for 45–60 seconds.</b> Use the visitor’s stated preference and availability, name each complete activity chunk, explain one schedule or access detail, ask one follow-up question, and make one polite invitation with <span class="ex">Would you like to...?</span></div>

